        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "09412cc0-e45c-5d59-b15e-896ead4724ee",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Gabriela Mistral, Alan Sillitoe, Geoffrey Chaucer, Thomas Wyatt, Ivo Andrić, Saint-John Perse, Miguel Ángel Asturias, Pablo Neruda, George Seferis, Czesław Miłosz, Octavio Paz\n- Concepts: Nobel Prize in Literature, angry young men, poet-diplomat\n\nFacts:\n- In 1945, Gabriela Mistral became the first Latin American author to receive the Nobel Prize in Literature.\n- Gabriela Mistral received the 1945 Nobel Prize in Literature for lyric poetry inspired by powerful emotions that made her name a symbol of the idealistic aspirations of the Latin American world.\n- Alan Sillitoe was born on 4 March 1928 and died on 25 April 2010.\n- Alan Sillitoe was an English writer and was considered one of the so-called angry young men of the 1950s.\n- Alan Sillitoe disliked the angry young men label, as did most of the other writers to whom it was applied.\n- Geoffrey Chaucer and Thomas Wyatt are among the best known poet-diplomats.\n- The poet-diplomat category includes Nobel Prize in Literature recipients Ivo Andrić, Gabriela Mistral, Saint-John Perse, Miguel Ángel Asturias, Pablo Neruda, George Seferis, Czesław Miłosz, and Octavio Paz.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
